WINDOWS SETUP GUIDE — Construction Waste Recovery Planner
============================================================

1. PREREQUISITES (install once)
------------------------------------------------------------
Python 3.10+  -> https://www.python.org/downloads/
                 (check "Add python.exe to PATH" during install)
Node.js 18+   -> https://nodejs.org/  (LTS installer)

Verify in Command Prompt:
    python --version
    node --version
    npm --version

2. TRANSFER THE PROJECT
------------------------------------------------------------
Copy the whole "construction-waste-recovery-planner" folder to this
machine (USB / network share / zip), or clone it:

    git clone https://github.com/ArinS19/construction-waste-recovery-planner.git

You can safely EXCLUDE these when copying — they are OS-specific /
regenerated automatically on first Windows run:

    backend\venv\
    backend\app\database\waste_planner.db
    backend\**\__pycache__\
    frontend\node_modules\
    frontend\dist\

DO keep backend\app\ml\saved_models\waste_recovery_model.joblib —
that is the already-trained ML model. It is a plain scikit-learn
pickle, not tied to Linux, and loads fine on Windows as long as
requirements.txt is installed here too (the launcher does this
automatically).

3. RUN IT (recommended — one command)
------------------------------------------------------------
From the project root, double-click:

    run_all.bat

This opens two console windows (backend + frontend). On the FIRST
run only, it will:
  - create backend\venv and pip install requirements.txt
  - run npm install for the frontend

That first run takes a few minutes and needs internet access — don't
close the console windows while it's working. Every run after that
is fast since venv/ and node_modules/ already exist.

If Windows Defender / firewall prompts about Python or Node, click
Allow (it's just binding to localhost).

3b. RUN IT MANUALLY (equivalent, two terminals)
------------------------------------------------------------
Terminal 1 (backend):
    cd backend
    python -m venv venv
    venv\Scripts\activate.bat
    pip install -r requirements.txt
    python -m uvicorn app.main:app --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8000 --reload

Terminal 2 (frontend):
    cd frontend
    npm install
    npm run dev -- --host 127.0.0.1 --port 5173

4. VERIFY IT WORKED
------------------------------------------------------------
Once both windows show "Application startup complete" / "ready":

    App:           http://127.0.0.1:5173
    API docs:      http://127.0.0.1:8000/docs
    Model status:  http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/ml/status
                   -> confirm "is_user_trained_model_loaded": true

Run a test assessment (New Assessment -> Concrete, Damaged, None,
500 kg) and confirm you get a pathway with a confidence score and
probability bars.

5. TROUBLESHOOTING
------------------------------------------------------------
- Bat window flashes and closes immediately:
    Open Command Prompt, cd into the project folder, run
    run_backend.bat directly (not by double-click) so the window
    stays open and you can read the actual error.

- "python" not recognized:
    Python wasn't added to PATH — reinstall and check that box, or
    use "py" instead of "python".

- "npm" not recognized:
    Same idea for Node.js — reinstall, check "Add to PATH".

- Model shows "is_user_trained_model_loaded": false:
    The .joblib file didn't make it over in the copy — recopy
    backend\app\ml\saved_models\ from the source machine.

6. STOPPING IT
------------------------------------------------------------
Close the two console windows, or press Ctrl+C in each and confirm
the "Terminate batch job" prompt.

------------------------------------------------------------
Nothing OS-specific lives in the app code itself (backend or
frontend) — only these launcher scripts differ between platforms.
The Linux/macOS equivalents are run_all.sh / run_backend.sh /
run_frontend.sh in the project root.
